  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.contract-test/no-error-map-is-built-outside-its-constructor}}
 {:seon.task/name "atoms-data"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-12: an atom is a proc not yet made (AGENTS.md section 3 step 4; docs/flow.md#the-step-fn-contract-every-proc-follows). The atoms and volatiles in data's namespaces, each classified (data, 2026-11-12), and its home. LOCAL ACCUMULATORS, which become values (a loop, a reduce, a map of delays): `seon.contract/within?` (left, a budget walked down), `seon.contract/kept` (left), `seon.contract/registry-of` and `seon.contract/registry-with` (compiled and reg, a lazy registry memoising each compiled schema: a map of delays, one per key, closed over by the registry value), `seon.vector/coarse-rows` (j, rows, es, a loop's counters), `seon.sqlite/replace-rows!` (done, a commit flag: try and finally say it), `seon.doc/baseline-reads` (cache, one read per [sha file]: a map built by reduce). A RUNNING THING'S STATE, which becomes a proc's: the text index (`seon.doc` indexes, a defonce atom of [at index counts readers] with reader counting in `seon.doc/acquire!` and `seon.doc/release!`, and `seon.sqlite/fts-open`'s per-handle atom of uses, retired and closed) is one owner's: an index proc holds the FTS handle, applies each write's rows (`seon.sqlite/replace-rows!`) in its step and answers a search on a channel, so no reader counts itself and no handle is closed under a reader; runtime made it one atom on 2026-11-12, so runtime's proc work decides where it lives. The embedding helper's handle in `seon.vector` (helper and facts, two volatiles in the seon.fx worker's handle) is that worker proc's state, returned by its step. A PROCESS FLAG: `seon.contract/private-checks`, set from :seon.config.contract/private-checked? at load and by `seon.contract/check-private!`, is a config value read once; check-private! goes. Order: the values first (no design question), then the flag, then the procs with runtime."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.contract-test/data-namespaces-hold-no-atom-but-a-procs}}
 {:seon.task/name "contracts-armed-at-definition"
  :seon.task/plan "Every definition happens once (docs/resolution.md). Built: seon.contract's defn, defn- and defchecked define a contracted var checked from its definition (`contracted-def`: the var holds `checked`, direct-linked and inlined callers call the checked body), and the contract-gap rule is one, `seon.merge/uncontracted`, which the writer's merge gate and root's judge both ask. Still armed after the definition, by alter-var-root: an agent's REPL defn (`seon.contract/arm!`, from seon.repl's definition handling), the program loaded from the db (seon.program calls `arm!` and `rearm!`), and the foundation namespaces at boot (`seon.contract/instrument!` over `boot-nss`, in seon.system); the ::wrapper metadata (`instrument-keys`) keeps the raw root such a wrapper wraps. Correct: a definition carrying :malli/schema is defined already checked, from its definition event, in those three places; arm!, rearm!, instrument! and the ::wrapper metadata go. The generated vars of defrecord/defprotocol are recognised from the definition events."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.contract-test/a-contracted-definition-is-checked-from-its-definition
                      seon.contract-test/one-contract-gap-rule-agrees-everywhere}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-contract-is-checked-once-and-costs-nothing"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit's finding that exact :calls bounds make a new contract a cost regression (8c30ac5600 and 9c8b3d016c deleted contracts to save calls): \"Oh yeah contracts don't count against anything as long as you do it through the optimal interface and we aren't checking it multiple times for no reason. So understand what instrumentation does ensure we are instrumenting them and then if that is covering it then we don't need to check it multiple times if it's the same data.\" (docs/decisions.md). Four steps, in order. (1) What instrumentation does: how seon.contract/defn and seon.contract/wrap check args, returns and arity, where it is on (:seon.config.contract/instrument, the seven boot namespaces armed by seon.contract/instrument!, agents by fork), and what it misses: fns defined outside the defn macro, private fns, multimethods, protocol methods, anything excluded; written in the contract section of the wiki. (2) One interface: every contract is checked through that instrumentation; hand m/validate and m/explain calls and other ad hoc checks in src go through it or are deleted where the instrumented contract already covers them; each gap found in (1) is filled, so every contracted fn is instrumented. (3) No double checks: the same data validated more than once for no reason (a return checked and then the same value as the next fn's arg under the same schema; a hand check beside an instrumented one; a value re-checked each pass of a loop) is a class: find the cases, then design the mechanism that removes it (not case patches), and bring the design to the owner, through the context session, before building it. (4) Only then seon.cost stops counting the contract's own calls in :calls (seon.contract/wrap's counted wrapper); every pinned number moves once, approved under this ruling (the 616 and 8 included), and the merge report lists each old and new number. After it, a new contract on the one interface costs nothing on any proof, and a redundant re-check is still waste. Docs: docs/cost.md and the contract section. (5) What one check costs (measured 2026-11-10, Context, for the owner's ruling \"Private helpers: tests only\" in docs/decisions.md): a contracted call costs about 70 engine ticks over its raw call (a small value's print 25.8k ticks with contracts on, 12.9k off); a compiled validator per argument should be a few ticks, so a check of a one-argument function with a simple schema should cost about 10. Find where the 70 go by profiling seon.contract/wrap's checked function on one call (the arity dispatch, the args vector, each argument's validator, the return's, seon.profile's counting, apply) and cut it at its cause, so that checking public functions on every call costs what a check is worth. The cost bounds that count checks rather than work (their calls fall tenfold with private checks off while their ticks stay the same: seon.ctx-test/a-callers-window-costs-its-forms 33 calls in test, 3 in production, 2.67M ticks either way; a-window-moves-by-its-change 21/2; the-patch-costs-the-change-not-the-session 21/2; a-lookups-patch-costs-its-change 9/1) are restated in ticks only with the owner's yes (step 4)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.contract-test/every-contracted-fn-is-instrumented seon.cost-test/a-contracts-own-calls-are-not-counted seon.contract-test/a-check-costs-its-validators}}
